<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Nördlingen</h1><p class="st-meta">1215 CE – 1803 CE · capital Nördlingen</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Walled Swabian imperial city inside the Ries meteorite crater, known for its Whitsun fair and the two battles of Nördlingen in the Thirty Years War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>A royal estate at Nördlingen in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordlinger-ries">Ries</a> basin, an ancient meteorite crater, passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-regensburg">Regensburg</a> and back to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a>. In 1215 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii">Emperor Frederick II</a> recognised it as an imperial city, and its growth was helped by its position on the roads between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franconia">Franconia</a>, Swabia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council dominated by merchants ruled the city. Its Whitsun fair was one of the leading markets of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, and cloth, leather and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> were its main trades. Nördlingen adopted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in the 1520s. The great hall church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-georg-nordlingen">St. Georg</a>, with its tall tower known as the Daniel, and an intact ring of walls testify to its wealth.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The city suffered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years War, when imperial and Spanish troops won the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nordlingen-1634">Battle of Nördlingen</a> in 1634 and French forces fought there again in 1645. It lost its freedom in 1803 to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="imperial city, 1215 CE – 1803 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden=